A||||5||ocalafiero1||Brake problem.....Long.....||||1||
Z||000000||ocalafiero1||09-20-2001||08:33 PM||||Ok, I checked the archives and have a few ideas, but want to bounce this one off of you guys (and gals). Last sunday the wife and I went out to check on our new house, on the way home (up to this point no problems or unusual pedal travel), at a red light, the person in the car next to us told me that my rear drivers side brake was "on fire". I immediately pulled into a parking lot, and got out to see not a fire, but a bit of smoke and heat shimmer coming out from behind the wheel. I put the car into neutral and made sure the e brake was released to try to roll the car to check for a stuck caliper. The car wouldn't roll at first so I pumped the brake pedal once and heard the caliper pop loose, and rolled it a few feet, hearing no scraping or rubbing. I jacked the car up, removed the (melting) center trim piece from the wheel, burned a hole in a Mechanix glove on the wheel stud and let everything cool off. As it was cooling, I heard a small "pop" from behind the wheel (or in that area). Since we were about 2 miles from home we let it cool down and then drove back. The brakes seemed to grab ok leaving the lot, but after a mile or so I tested them and the pedal went most of the way to the floor (75-80%) before stopping the car. Tonight I went out to take a look at it and with the car off, the pedal stays somewhat spongy. With the car running there is not a lot of increase in pedal pressure, but when I let off the brake pedal, the engine bogs a little, and makes a rapid "puff,puff,puff" sound and the rpms drop while this is happening. The fluid resivoir for the MC is full and there is no leaking fluid under the car, So I am assuming no leaks. My guess is maybe the vacuum line going to the brake booster, or amaster cylinder problem. The calipers are going to get replaced for sure, but they didn't lock up at all anymore on the way home that day. I did disconnect the ebrake cable at that wheel before driving it home that day after verifying that it released when releasing with the handle in the car. Any help you may have would be appreceated.<P><BR>Thanks,<P>Mike<P>Ocala, Florida||||reg||1
Z||000001||Jncomutt||09-21-2001||07:26 AM||||Well I'm not too sure on your case but heres somethings I've picked up reading other threads.  As far as the brake pedal feeling 'spongy' since the incident, my guess would be that it boiled the brake fluid and now has little air bubbles which are compressing when you press the pedal.  Bleed the brakes and it should solve that.  <P>And as far as the brakes not releasing, you might want to make sure the slide bolts there, the metal sleeves, are well lubricated and move easily.  If they are binded within the caliper, that could be the source of your issue.<P>Like I said, I could be wrong, just going by what I've heard, hope it helps.||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||09-21-2001||08:48 PM||||I have no doubt you've boiled the fluid.<P>I'd recomend replacing the affected caliper, all it's hardware and both rotors. (New pads...) Odds are good the over heated rotor is warping. It will get progressivly worse over time. Once they get this hot I won't trust them. You can turn out a slight warp but you can't undo the heat damage to the metal.<P>I like Permatex #20355 brake grease but any good Brake grease will work. Never Ever use any other grease on brake parts.<P>Flush the entire system with new fluid. Expect to use 2 quarts of fluid at least. There's notes that may help with that in my cave.<P>I'd also do the manual adjustment in me cave. It solves any issue about the calipers being at the correct clearance right away. I've learned you just can't trust this to the handbrake and autoadjusting.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/halfpole.gif"> <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Wannabe||09-21-2001||09:04 PM||||Make sure your parking brake cables are free as well.||||reg||1
Z||000004||ocalafiero1||09-21-2001||11:11 PM||||I'll start pulling everything apart in the morning. Definately going to change the pads, calipers and rotors on the rear (both sides. I did the fronts when I got the car last fall. A question about replacing all of the fluid, though. Will it just be a matter of "bleeding" the new fluid through the system by topping it off at the master cyl as I bleed the calipers, or do I need to remove the master cyl and bench bleed it? I'm also going to check for a vacuum leak between the engine and the booster, as the putting sound upon releaseing the break pedal has me thinking that there's a problem there also. Thanks Ogre, I'll check your site in a few.<P>Mike Johansson<BR>Ocala, Florida||||reg||1
Z||000005||theogre||09-22-2001||02:50 PM||||You don't need to bench bleed unless you think you got air in the MC.<P>Pull as much old fluid from the MC as you can before you start. use a "turkey baster" or similar squeeze ball type thing. don't try to get every drop. wipe up dirt in the tank floor with a soft rag. fill it with new fluid. DO NOT pour the fluid directly over the MC openings! you can force air into it if you do.<P>before you tear it apart. flush the fronts as I described in my cave.<P>fill and cap the MC. (At the least set the cover on to keep trash from falling into it. but you really want to close it anytime you aren't actually cleaning or filling it.)<P>Remove the left rear caliper and flush the line untill new fluid flows. <P><B>Make certain the MC is not allowed to run dry!</B><P>install the new left caliper and let it fill with fluid. don't worry about a full bleed job yet. just let the fluid gravity feed. it will take longer but the caliper should fill with less trapped air.<P>Now go do the same on the other side, allowing new fluid to run from the line before installing the new caliper.<P>Once it's back together and you've got fluid filled in, then bleed it.<P>expect to use 2 quarts of fluid for the flush/bleed job. Try to find the Valvoline synthetic fluid. It's inexpensive and outperforms most other DOT-3 and DOT-4.<P>I'd also recomend the manual adjustment procedure. This take all the guesswork out of replacing the rear calipers. It will set proper clearance even on cars with nonoperation hand brakes.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 09-22-2001).]||||reg||1
